<header><p class="enc-kicker">Place · city</p><h1 class="enc-title">Bologna</h1></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Bologna is a city in northern <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/italy">Italy</a>, home to one of the oldest universities in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/europe">Europe</a>. From the eleventh century its scholars studied the recovered <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/corpus-juris-civilis">Corpus Juris Civilis</a>, the law code compiled under Justinian. The teaching of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/roman-law">Roman law</a> at Bologna made it a channel through which the legal legacy of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/byzantine-empire">Byzantine Empire</a> shaped European <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/civil-law">civil law</a>.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/bologna.jpg" alt="Bologna" width="320" height="213" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Fabio Ciminelli · CC BY-SA 4.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Torri_di_Bologna,_Bologna.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Setting</h2><p>Bologna lies on the edge of the Po Plain at the foot of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/apennines">Apennine Mountains</a>, where the valleys of the Reno and Savena rivers meet. Its two main watercourses flow directly to the sea, so the town stands outside the drainage basin of the River Po. It is the capital and largest city of the Emilia-<a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/romagna">Romagna</a> region in northern <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/italy">Italy</a>.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">History</h2><p>Traces of human habitation go back to the 3rd millennium BCE, with significant settlements from about the 9th century BCE, in the Villanovan culture. Etruscan influence reached the area in the 7th to 6th centuries BCE, and by the end of the 6th century the Etruscan city of Felsina stood on the site. By the 4th century BCE it was held by the Gaulish Boii, and in 196 BCE it became a Roman colony and municipium named Bonōnia. In the last years of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/western-roman-empire">Western Roman Empire</a> the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/goths">Goths</a> repeatedly sacked it. According to ancient chronicles, Bishop Petronius then rebuilt the ruined town and founded the basilica of Saint Stephen. He is still revered as the patron saint of Bologna.</p><p>In the Middle Ages Bologna was a free municipality and later a signoria, and was among the largest European cities by population. Papal rule lasted from 1506 to 1796, a period that historians have generally judged one of severe decline, though the 1500s brought major developments. Emperor <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/charles-v-holy-roman-emperor">Charles V</a> was crowned there in 1530, the last emperor to be crowned by the pope. In 1564 the Archiginnasio, the main building of the university, was built, and the Bolognese School of painters, including Annibale Carracci, Domenichino and Guercino, arose in these years. The city then had ninety-six convents, more than any other Italian city.</p><p><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/napoleon">Napoleon</a> entered Bologna on 19 June 1796, and the city passed through the Cispadane Republic and the Cisalpine Republic to the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kingdom-of-italy">Kingdom of Italy</a>. The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/congress-of-vienna">Congress of Vienna</a> of 1815 restored it to the Papal States. Papal rule was contested in the uprisings of 1831, when the rebel provinces planned to unite with Bologna as their capital; Pope Gregory XVI asked for Austrian help, and Austrian forces defeated the rebellion by 26 April.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">The place today</h2><p>The University of Bologna, conventionally said to have been founded in 1088 by the glossators <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/irnerius">Irnerius</a> and Pepo, is the oldest university in continuous operation. Its Studium began as a loosely organised system in which each master collected fees from students individually, and colleges were founded to support students of a particular nationality. It attracted scholars from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/italy">Italy</a> and across <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/europe">Europe</a> and was an important centre of intellectual life in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/middle-ages">Middle Ages</a>. The city is known for its towers, churches and long porticoes, and in 2021 <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/unesco">UNESCO</a> recognised the porticoes as a World Heritage Site.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Bologna” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>The Corpus Juris Civilis, rediscovered in late eleventh-century Italy and taught at <b>Bologna</b> and the universities that followed its model, became the foundation of European civil law and, through it, of the legal systems of most of the world outside the common law tradition.</blockquote><a href="/empire/byzantine-empire">Byzantine Empire · Global &amp; Regional Impact</a></li><li><blockquote>He had already taken Verona and Vicenza in 1387 and Padua in 1388; he then acquired Pisa in 1399, received the submission of Siena and Perugia in 1399–1400 and took <b>Bologna</b> in 1402.</blockquote><a href="/empire/duchy-of-milan">Duchy of Milan · Foundation</a></li><li><blockquote>The Roman Campagna was given over to great estates and grazing, increasingly malarial and thinly populated, while the Marche and Umbria grew grain, wine and olives, and <b>Bologna</b>, the richest city of the state, had a large silk industry and Europe’s oldest university.</blockquote><a href="/empire/papal-states">Papal States · Socioeconomy</a></li><li><blockquote>From about 900 BCE the Villanovan culture of the early Iron Age, named after a cemetery at Villanova near <b>Bologna</b> excavated in 1853, appears on the plateaus where the great Etruscan cities later stood.</blockquote><a href="/culture/etruscans">Etruscans · Origins</a></li><li><blockquote>Rome kept the last Jewish ghetto in Western Europe, and in 1858 papal police in <b>Bologna</b> took the six-year-old Edgardo Mortara from his Jewish parents because a servant claimed to have baptised him secretly.</blockquote><a href="/government/theocracy">Theocracy · Weaknesses</a></li><li><blockquote>When Toscanini refused again at a concert in <b>Bologna</b> in May 1931, Blackshirts assaulted him, and he left Italy until after the Second World War.</blockquote><a href="/songs/giovinezza">Giovinezza</a></li></ul></section>
